WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:19.720
<v Ira Glass>A couple years ago, Erin went to live in Poland. And she went with certain prejudices. She was Jewish, and she was scared of anti-Semitism. Now, many Poles will tell you that this prejudice is completely unfair. But Erin lost Polish family members in the Holocaust. And Poland's where some of the most infamous concentration camps operated, Auschwitz and Birkenau and Treblinka.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，艾琳去了波兰生活。她带着某些偏见而去。她是犹太人，且对反犹太主义感到恐惧。现在，许多波兰人会告诉你，这种偏见完全是不公平的。但艾琳在犹太人大屠杀中失去了波兰的家人。而波兰正是奥斯威辛、比克瑙和特雷布林卡等最臭名昭著的集中营所在地。

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:44.630
<v Erin Einhorn>I'd only heard one thing about Poland my entire life, from my mother, from my grandparents, that they had always hated Jews. And they had always wanted to see Jews killed. And then, when the Holocaust started to happen, they were happy to see it happen, and they were collaborators. Even though my mother was saved by a Pole, that was always told to me as, well, she only did it for the money.
<v Erin Einhorn>我这辈子只从我母亲和祖父母那里听说过一件事，那就是他们一直憎恨犹太人。他们一直希望看到犹太人被杀。当大屠杀开始时，他们很高兴看到这一切发生，并且他们是通敌者。尽管我母亲被一位波兰人救了，但这事一直被描述成：嗯，她那样做只是为了钱。

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:50.120
<v Ira Glass>So you were scared about anti-Semitism there. What did you find?
<v Ira Glass>所以你当时对那里的反犹太主义感到害怕。你发现了什么？

00:00:50.120 --> 00:01:12.510
<v Erin Einhorn>Well, the first thing I found is that I had a roommate with a Hebrew letter tattooed on her shoulder. I found the old Jewish quarter in Krakow, which is where I was living, has been converted into this kind of Jewish theme park. There's Jewish-themed cafes that have matzoh ball soup and latkes, all this Jewish food.
<v Erin Einhorn>嗯，我发现的第一件事是，我的室友肩膀上纹着一个希伯来字母。我发现我居住的克拉科夫的老犹太区，已经被改造成了一种犹太主题公园。那里有提供无酵饼汤和土豆饼等所有犹太食物的犹太主题咖啡馆。

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:37.240
<v Erin Einhorn>And they play it with klezmer music. And there's all these pictures of Jews with black hats on the walls. And there's this huge enthusiasm for Jewish culture. And I went to a nightclub with a friend, and the nightclub singer, as part of a set that included all kinds of jazz songs and "When the Saints Go Marching In," all of a sudden started playing Fiddler on the Roof songs.
<v Erin Einhorn>他们还放着克莱兹默音乐。墙上到处都是戴着黑帽的犹太人的照片。人们对犹太文化有着巨大的热情。我和朋友去了一家夜总会，夜总会的歌手在表演包含各种爵士歌曲和《圣徒列队前进》的曲目时，突然开始演奏《屋顶上的提琴手》中的歌曲。

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:43.810
<v Ira Glass>Now, you brought tapes of this. Here's a recording recorded in the nightclub. Just describe this nightclub.
<v Ira Glass>你带了这些录音带。这是在夜总会录下的录音。描述一下这家夜总会。

00:01:43.810 --> 00:02:38.680
<v Erin Einhorn>It's a nightclub that appeals to the up-and-coming yuppie set of Krakow, 20-, 30-something, really beautiful people in really tight clothes. All the women have midriff-baring, and men are in leather pants, and Euro hipster. When we walked in, she was singing "Yesterday," by the Beatles. But she does a whole diverse set. But her climax, the thing she builds to, is this sort of montage of Jewish music. And the climax of the whole thing is "Hava Nagila."
<v Erin Einhorn>那是一家吸引克拉科夫新兴雅皮士群体的夜总会，大概20到30多岁，都是穿着紧身衣服的俊男美女。女人们都露着腹部，男人们穿着皮裤，是那种欧式时髦风格。我们进去时，她正在唱披头士乐队的《Yesterday》。但她的表演曲目非常多样。而她表演的最高潮，也就是她极力渲染的，是某种犹太音乐串烧。整个表演的高潮就是《Hava Nagila》。

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:46.540
<v Ira Glass>We should say to people who have never been to a Jewish wedding, this is the old-school, dance at a bar mitzvah, dance at a wedding kind of song.
<v Ira Glass>我们要对那些从没参加过犹太婚礼的人说一下，这是一种老派的、在犹太教成人礼或婚礼上跳舞时才会听到的歌。

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.180
<v Erin Einhorn>And they were just crazy for it. They loved it, loved it.
<v Erin Einhorn>他们简直为之疯狂。他们爱死这首歌了，非常喜欢。

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.960
<v Ira Glass>And Krakow is how close to Auschwitz, the concentration camp?
<v Ira Glass>克拉科夫离奥斯威辛集中营有多近？

00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:19.870
<v Erin Einhorn>It's about an hour's drive, maybe a little less than an hour. A friend of mine, an American Jew visiting me in Krakow, he'd spent the day at Auschwitz. And then I took him to this nightclub. And when the singer starts singing-- first, she's singing Fiddler on the Roof songs, and he thought that was pretty funny because, here, it's a Broadway take on the old country right here in the middle of the old country. And that was sort of kitschy and funny.
<v Erin Einhorn>开车大约一个小时，也许不到一个小时。我的一位正在克拉科夫探望我的美国犹太朋友，他花了一整天在奥斯威辛。然后我带他去了这家夜总会。当歌手开始唱歌时——起初，她唱的是《屋顶上的提琴手》里的歌，他觉得这很有趣，因为百老汇版本的旧国度音乐，竟然就在旧国度的中心响起。那感觉既俗气又好笑。

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:52.520
<v Erin Einhorn>And then she started singing "Hava Nagila." And everybody's like, wooooo! And they got all excited, and they jumped to their feet. And they're belly-dancing, and they're singing along. And my friend turned to me, and he says, I'm sorry. Where was I eight hours ago?
<v Erin Einhorn>然后她开始唱《Hava Nagila》。每个人都欢呼着“噢——！”他们变得非常兴奋，跳了起来。他们在跳肚皮舞，还跟着一起唱。我朋友转向我，说：“对不起，我八小时前在哪儿？”

00:03:52.520 --> 00:04:09.740
<v Ira Glass>It can throw you when somebody you thought was your enemy shows up to what you thought was going to be a fight dressed as you, wearing your favorite suit, and those shoes that you thought really only looked good on you. It's hard to throw the first punch in that situation. It's hard to know whether you should stop hating them or not.
<v Ira Glass>当那个你以为是敌人的人，打扮成你的样子，穿着你最喜欢的西装，穿着你以为只有你穿才好看的鞋子，出现在你以为会发生争斗的地方时，这真的会让你感到错愕。在这种情况下，很难挥出第一拳。也很难判断自己到底该不该继续恨他们。

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:21.930
<v Ira Glass>After all, they're trying so hard to be like you. It's almost flattering. Or maybe you should hate them all the more. I mean, the sheer gall of it. There they are, you.
<v Ira Glass>毕竟，他们那么努力地想成为你。这几乎令人受宠若惊。或者也许你应该更加恨他们。我是说，这得有多大的胆量啊。他们就在那儿，他们就是你。

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:42.630
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, Fake ID. We bring you stories of people traveling under fake papers, false identities, not for money, not for power, not for personal gain, but only for their own pleasure and amusement, which, I have to say, makes the whole thing all the more confusing. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目的主题是“假身份”。我们为您讲述人们使用假证件、假身份旅行的故事，不是为了钱，不是为了权力，也不是为了个人私利，仅仅是为了他们自己的快乐和消遣，我必须说，这让整件事变得更加令人困惑。这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》，由公共广播国际发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:04:42.630 --> 00:05:20.330
<v Ira Glass>Act One of our program today, Pole Vault. In that act, the very confusing story of a Jew in Krakow among non-Jews who think that Judaism is just kind of cool. Act Two, This Blessed House. In that act, a story from Jhumpa Lahiri about an Indian couple that keeps finding trinkets from a religion that is not theirs, which you think would not really cause much trouble. Act Three, The Lie That Binds. David Sedaris explains how to stay in love. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天节目的第一幕，“撑杆跳”。在这一幕中，讲述了一个关于在克拉科夫的犹太人，身处一群认为犹太教很酷的非犹太人之中的困惑故事。第二幕，“这个受祝福的家”。在这一幕中，讲述了Jhumpa Lahiri创作的一个关于一对印度夫妇的故事，他们不断发现一些不属于他们宗教的小饰品，你以为这不会引起什么麻烦。第三幕，“束缚的谎言”。大卫·塞达里斯解释了如何保持爱情。请继续收听。

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:41.420
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Pole Vault. So, Erin Einhorn went to Poland, to find the Catholic family that had sheltered and saved her mother from the concentration camps during World War II. And she found that in Krakow, where she was living, in a country where the Jewish population had been exterminated, Judaism was suddenly trendy, hip even, for all sorts of reasons.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“撑杆跳”。艾琳·艾因霍恩去了波兰，去寻找那个在二战期间保护并挽救她母亲免遭集中营厄运的天主教家庭。她发现，在她居住的克拉科夫，在一个犹太人口已被灭绝的国家，犹太文化突然变得时髦，甚至是潮流，原因有很多。

00:05:41.420 --> 00:06:09.530
<v Erin Einhorn>Jews comprised, at one point in time, 10% of the population and were living throughout that country. So there's cemeteries, buildings. There's just Hebrew letters etched in stone that once said "pharmacy" in Yiddish. Yet most people under 60 have never met a Jew. So when they hear about it, when they get a piece of it, they're excited about it. They're fascinated by it. They're drawn to it.
<v Erin Einhorn>犹太人曾经占人口的10%，遍布全国。所以那里有墓地、建筑。石头上刻着希伯来字母，曾经用依地语写着“药房”。然而，大多数60岁以下的人从未见过犹太人。所以当他们听到关于犹太人的事，接触到一点相关文化时，他们会感到兴奋。他们对此着迷，被其吸引。

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:15.550
<v Ira Glass>Right, because there are these clues all around, and they feel like, oh, finally, that's what that means.
<v Ira Glass>没错，因为到处都是这些线索，他们感觉，“噢，原来如此，原来这就是那个意思。”

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:59.690
<v Erin Einhorn>The Polish national television, still under communism, played Fiddler on the Roof at some point in the late '80s on television. And when I was doing interviews this year, people constantly referred to, oh, the first time I saw Fiddler on the Roof. It was this thing that-- their grandmothers would gather them around the television and say, "Oh, look, look, look. That's what it looked like. These are the people I grew up with. It looked like this." One of the other clips I think you have is of a woman who's actually writing her master's thesis about Isaac Bashevis Singer, and is taking Yiddish, and is fascinated by Jewish culture, who the first time she experienced Jewish culture was when they ran Fiddler on the Roof on television.
<v Erin Einhorn>波兰国家电视台在80年代末期的某个时候播放过《屋顶上的提琴手》。当我今年做采访时，人们不断提到：“噢，我第一次看《屋顶上的提琴手》的时候。”这就是那种——他们的祖母会把他们围在电视机前说：“噢，看，看，看。这就是当年的样子。这就是我一起长大的人。当时就是这个样子的。”我认为你手头还有一段剪辑，是一个正在写关于艾萨克·巴什维斯·辛格硕士论文的女性，她正在学依地语，对犹太文化着迷，她第一次体验犹太文化就是当电视上播放《屋顶上的提琴手》的时候。

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:01.450
<v Ira Glass>Here's that clip.
<v Ira Glass>这是那段剪辑。

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:23.650
<v Woman>My first contact was quite a long time ago, when I first saw Fiddler on the Roof. And it was in the times when we didn't have a VCR at home, so my father would record it on a tape recorder. So I had three hours, and I would listen to that again and again, so that after some time, I knew it by heart.
<v Woman>我第一次接触是很久以前，我第一次看《屋顶上的提琴手》。那时候家里还没有录像机，所以我父亲用磁带录音机录了下来。我有三个小时的录音，我不停地反复听，过了一段时间，我都能背下来了。

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:54.820
<v Woman>And then whenever I had some difficult times, I would go back to the cassette and listen to that, to the songs, and to the humor, and the culture, the different-- well, different to what I've been taught before. I was taught on communist history books. And you had no mention about Jewish people living in Poland. You just said one word about the king Casimir the Great, who invited Jews in the 14th century, then big nothing, and then the Holocaust.
<v Woman>每当我遇到困难的时候，我都会回去听那盘磁带，听那些歌，听其中的幽默，还有文化，那是与——嗯，与我以前受到的教育所不同的文化。我受的是共产主义历史书的教育。书里根本没提犹太人在波兰的生活。只提到了一句卡齐米日大帝在14世纪邀请犹太人，然后就什么都没了，直接跳到大屠杀。

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.340
<v Ira Glass>How many Jews were in Poland before the war?
<v Ira Glass>战前波兰有多少犹太人？

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:00.470
<v Erin Einhorn>In Poland, I think about 3.5 million.
<v Erin Einhorn>在波兰，我想大约有350万。

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.600
<v Ira Glass>And now?
<v Ira Glass>现在呢？

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:15.030
<v Erin Einhorn>Now it's about 3,000, something like that. And in Krakow, which is where I spent most of my time last year, there's about 170 Jews, most of them over 80.
<v Erin Einhorn>现在大约有3000人，差不多这样。在克拉科夫，也就是我去年待得最久的地方，大约有170名犹太人，大多数都超过80岁了。

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.890
<v Erin Einhorn>[KLEZMER MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Erin Einhorn>[克莱兹默音乐播放]

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:23.320
<v Ira Glass>Erin, what's this recording of?
<v Ira Glass>艾琳，这是关于什么的录音？

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:40.390
<v Erin Einhorn>Yeah, this is the annual Jewish cultural festival in Krakow. And in a country with almost no Jews, it's really an extraordinary thing. The clip you're playing is the final concert of the festival at which, the Polish newspapers reported this year, there were 10,000 participants.
<v Erin Einhorn>这是克拉科夫一年一度的犹太文化节。在一个几乎没有犹太人的国家，这确实是一件非同寻常的事情。你播放的片段是电影节闭幕音乐会，据波兰媒体今年报道，有10,000人参加。

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:58.470
<v Erin Einhorn>It's an outdoor concert right in the center of what had been the Jewish quarter. And they set up a stage on one end, decorated with menorahs and other Jewish symbols, and it's nationally televised. Polish national television carries this concert live.
<v Erin Einhorn>这是一场就在曾经的犹太区中心举行的户外音乐会。他们在舞台一端搭设了台子，装饰着灯台和其他犹太符号，并且进行全国直播。波兰国家电视台直播了这场音乐会。

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:23.260
<v Erin Einhorn>And they bring in klezmer musicians from around the world, the top klezmer musicians in the business. And they put them on stage, and they bring in thousands and thousands of young people, mostly in their 20s, teens, 20s, 30s, and they just pack this square. And they go crazy. It's just like this big rock concert, only it's klezmer music.
<v Erin Einhorn>他们请来了世界各地的顶级克莱兹默音乐家。他们把他们请上台，吸引了成千上万的年轻人，主要是20多岁、十几岁、30岁的人，把整个广场挤得水泄不通。他们陷入了疯狂。就像一场大型摇滚音乐会，只不过是克莱兹默音乐。

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:27.900
<v Ira Glass>And they're non-Jews. These people in the crowd, just to be clear, are non-Jews.
<v Ira Glass>他们是非犹太人。要说清楚的是，人群中的这些人都是非犹太人。

00:09:27.900 --> 00:10:03.930
<v Erin Einhorn>There might be some tourists there. There's a mix of people. But by and large, this is the youth culture of Krakow. But this concert capped a week of, there were classes on Jewish dancing that drew 200 people to a high school gym, 200 people who really wanted to learn the horah. There were 60 mostly college-aged kids in a workshop on the Yiddish language.
<v Erin Einhorn>可能有一些游客。人群是混合的。但总的来说，这是克拉科夫的青年文化。但这只是为期一周的活动的压轴戏，还有吸引了200人到高中体育馆学习犹太舞蹈的课程，200人真的很想学会霍拉舞。在依地语研讨会上，还有60名主要是大学年龄的孩子。

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:11.210
<v Ira Glass>Give your best understanding of how this trend came about. How did this happen?
<v Ira Glass>请谈谈你对这种趋势是如何形成的理解。这是怎么发生的？

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:25.190
<v Erin Einhorn>It was sort of an intellectual thing in the 80s. There was this group of people who were interested in it. And they would have meetings about Jewish culture, and bring in Jewish writers to come speak, or have concerts of Jewish music. But it was a small thing.
<v Erin Einhorn>这在80年代是一种知识分子风潮。有一群人对它很感兴趣。他们会举办关于犹太文化的会议，请犹太作家来演讲，或者举办犹太音乐会。但这只是小范围的。

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:48.030
<v Erin Einhorn>And from this group emerged a number of things, such as the first Jewish cultural festival, which actually started as a Jewish film festival in, I think, 1988. And what happened is it was a huge success. They expected a few dozen people. And in fact, a couple hundred came. And it was a full room. And that was 1988.
<v Erin Einhorn>从这个群体中产生了一些事情，比如第一个犹太文化节，实际上始于1988年的一个犹太电影节。结果非常成功。他们原以为只有几十人参加。实际上，有几百人来了。房间坐满了。那是1988年。

00:10:48.030 --> 00:11:11.680
<v Erin Einhorn>And the following year, communism falls. So suddenly, not only is there interest, but now there's opportunity. And actually, and then the big turning point around 1993, Spielberg comes to make Schindler's List. And suddenly, there's this big American director in Krakow, making a movie.
<v Erin Einhorn>第二年，共产主义垮台了。所以突然之间，不仅有了兴趣，现在还有了机会。事实上，然后在1993年左右出现了一个大转折，斯皮尔伯格来拍摄《辛德勒的名单》。突然间，一位美国大导演在克拉科夫拍电影。

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.440
<v Ira Glass>A movie about the Jews.
<v Ira Glass>一部关于犹太人的电影。

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:31.870
<v Erin Einhorn>And he's filming it in the Jewish quarter. He turned this little Jewish quarter into the set for his movie. And it was a huge success. And then when the movie comes out, all these people start flocking to this part of town to see where it was that Spielberg made this big movie, Schindler's List.
<v Erin Einhorn>他就在犹太区取景。他把这个小小的犹太区变成了电影的拍摄地。非常成功。电影上映后，人们开始蜂拥而至这个城镇，看看斯皮尔伯格拍摄那部大片《辛德勒的名单》的地方。

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:37.660
<v Ira Glass>Now, one of the pieces of tape you've brought in that you recorded there is with the owner of a bookshop?
<v Ira Glass>你带来的一段录音是关于一家书店老板的？

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:57.960
<v Erin Einhorn>Right, right. And he was a guy who had this love of Jewish culture, and he had just opened up this bookshop. And he put Jewish book titles on the shelves. And then one day, this movie gets made, and the whole world changes.
<v Erin Einhorn>对，对。他是一个热爱犹太文化的人，刚开了这家书店。他在书架上放了犹太书籍。然后有一天，这部电影拍出来了，整个世界都变了。

00:11:57.960 --> 00:11:59.540
<v Ira Glass>Here we go. Here's the tape.
<v Ira Glass>开始吧。这是录音。

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:37.460
<v Bookshop Owner>We should build memorial for Spielberg in the center of this place. Because thanks to Spielberg, we can exist now in better situation. After Spielberg's film, it was a kind of revolution in the mentality of people. You know that premiere of this film was in December, in New York. First days of January, '94, first American tourists visited me in the bookshop. And every day, it was the same.
<v Bookshop Owner>我们应该在广场中心为斯皮尔伯格建一座纪念碑。因为多亏了斯皮尔伯格，我们现在的情况好多了。在斯皮尔伯格的电影之后，人们的心理发生了一种革命。你知道这部电影在纽约的首映是在12月。1994年1月的前几天，第一批美国游客拜访了我的书店。每天都是如此。

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:55.380
<v Erin Einhorn>The bookstore started giving tours. First, they wrote a guidebook of where all the places in the movie were. And then the tourists were coming, and the locals would start to come. It wasn't just foreigners. The whole city wanted to come. And Poles who came to visit from other parts of the country would want to come.
<v Erin Einhorn>书店开始组织旅游团。首先，他们写了一本指南，标出电影中所有的拍摄地点。接着游客来了，当地人也开始来了。不仅是外国人。整个城市的人都想来。从全国各地来访的波兰人也想来。

00:12:55.380 --> 00:13:13.420
<v Erin Einhorn>And actually, it was so successful, before the movie, there was just the bookshop and one cafe, called the Ariel Cafe. After the movie came out, it was such a success, the people who owned the building where the Ariel Cafe was actually evicted the people who had started the cafe.
<v Erin Einhorn>实际上，它非常成功，在电影上映前，只有书店和一家叫Ariel的咖啡馆。电影上映后，太成功了，拥有Ariel咖啡馆大楼的人竟然驱逐了经营咖啡馆的人。

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:14.500
<v Ira Glass>The Jewish cafe.
<v Ira Glass>那家犹太咖啡馆。

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:47.360
<v Erin Einhorn>They were kicked out, and he opened his own Ariel Cafe. And they rented the building next door and opened their own Ariel Cafe. So for the better part of the 1990s, you had two Ariels there. And then there was a lawsuit, and one of them subsequently changed its name to the Olive Cafe. Now it's a whole thriving industry. There's actual Jewish gift shops in Krakow, where they sell wood carvings of Jews playing the violin.
<v Erin Einhorn>他们被赶了出来，房东自己开了Ariel咖啡馆。他们租了隔壁的楼，也开了自己的Ariel咖啡馆。所以90年代的大部分时间里，那里有两个Ariel。后来发生了诉讼，其中一家随后更名为Olive咖啡馆。现在这是一个欣欣向荣的产业。克拉科夫有真正的犹太礼品店，在那里他们出售拉小提琴的犹太人的木雕。

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:54.840
<v Ira Glass>And how are you feeling about this, as a Jew whose family had to flee Poland in the Second World War?
<v Ira Glass>作为一名家人在二战期间被迫逃离波兰的犹太人，你对此有何感想？

00:13:54.840 --> 00:14:14.060
<v Erin Einhorn>Well, at first, I was somewhat offended, actually, on the grounds of, OK, now that we're not here, now they're interested in Jewish culture? And I wondered, well, gosh, are people making money from this? Are they marketing my culture? It's this weird thing.
<v Erin Einhorn>嗯，起初，我确实有点被冒犯了，因为，好吧，现在我们不在了，他们才对犹太文化感兴趣？我心想，天哪，人们是在靠这个赚钱吗？他们是在营销我的文化吗？这真是一种奇怪的感觉。

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:30.250
<v Erin Einhorn>I hadn't been appropriated before. So on one hand, you're kind of flattered. And on the other hand, there's just something about it that doesn't sit right.
<v Erin Einhorn>我以前没有经历过这种文化挪用。所以一方面，你感到有点受宠若惊。另一方面，这其中就是有什么东西让你感觉不对劲。

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:52.260
<v Ira Glass>I wonder if the feeling you're feeling is something that happens to other groups a lot. I would imagine that an older black person visiting the House of Blues, which is, if I understand right, mostly white-owned and white audiences there walking in with a kind of kitschy presentation of black music.
<v Ira Glass>我想知道，你现在的这种感觉是否在其他群体中也很常见。我可以想象一位年长的黑人走进“蓝调之屋”，据我所知，那主要由白人拥有，观众也主要是白人，带着一种对黑人音乐的俗气展示走进去。

00:14:52.260 --> 00:15:09.040
<v Erin Einhorn>I often compared it to Santa Fe. You have a culture that's primarily selling, marketing Native American culture. And it isn't Native Americans necessarily doing the marketing. There may be some involved, but primarily, their culture is being packaged and sold as part of a tour to tourists.
<v Erin Einhorn>我经常把它比作圣达菲。你有一个主要是销售、营销美洲原住民文化的文化。而且进行营销的未必是美洲原住民。可能有一些人参与其中，但主要是他们的文化被包装并作为旅游项目出售给游客。

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:12.130
<v Ira Glass>Right, after we've driven them off the land.
<v Ira Glass>没错，在我们把他们赶出土地之后。

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:13.730
<v Erin Einhorn>Exactly.
<v Erin Einhorn>正是如此。

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:17.530
<v Ira Glass>You brought in another clip of tape from your interviews in Poland, a woman named Eva?
<v Ira Glass>你带来了你在波兰采访的另一段录音，一个叫伊娃的女人？

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:36.470
<v Erin Einhorn>Yeah, Ewa Nowakowska. She's an English translator. Her perspective on it really reflects the romanticism that this younger generation has. Her grandmother had described for her how beautiful the Jewish culture was and how the Jewish quarter would light up.
<v Erin Einhorn>对，伊娃·诺瓦科夫斯卡。她是一位英语翻译。她对这件事的看法确实反映了这一代年轻人所具有的浪漫主义。她的祖母向她描述了犹太文化是多么美丽，以及犹太区曾经是如何灯火辉煌。

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:38.630
<v Ira Glass>Here, let's hear that tape.
<v Ira Glass>来，听听那段录音。

00:15:38.630 --> 00:16:25.120
<v Ewa Nowakowska>I think that there is a strong feeling of regret whenever I just roam along the streets of Kazimierz, of the former Jewish district, because I can realize how many people were just crowding the streets, how many small shops were opened. It was a thriving culture. And my grandmother's sister told me about all the festivals, especially about Shabbat on Saturdays, when she-- she can recall the whole thing. She could just walk along the streets. And she could see the lit windows in which there were the candlesticks, and they started singing all the Jewish songs. And it was very-- even for the Catholics, it was incredible to feel and experience. And then everything perished, everything disappeared.
<v Ewa Nowakowska>我想，每当我漫步在卡齐米日的街道，也就是以前的犹太区，我都会有一种强烈的遗憾感，因为我能意识到曾经有多少人挤在街上，有多少小店开着。那是一种蓬勃发展的文化。我祖母的妹妹告诉我所有那些节日，特别是星期六的安息日，当她——她能回忆起这一切。她可以在街上走。她能看到窗户里亮着的烛台，他们开始唱所有的犹太歌曲。这真是——即使是对天主教徒来说，去感受和体验这种氛围也是不可思议的。然后一切都毁灭了，一切都消失了。

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:58.480
<v Erin Einhorn>And this was this woman who had actually-- she's a poet-- and she had actually written a series of poems as a tribute to the Jewish family that used to live next door to her grandmother. And she has her grandmother's old diary, and the Jewish family that lived next door had written some Hebrew letters in the book or something. And she talks about it in these romantic terms as this is this last relic, this last holdover, the last thing we have from this family. Whether or not that's true, whether or not that family even survived, we don't necessarily know.
<v Erin Einhorn>这就是那个女人，她是一位诗人——她实际上写了一系列诗歌，以纪念曾经住在她祖母隔壁的犹太家庭。她保存着她祖母的旧日记，那个隔壁的犹太家庭在书里写了一些希伯来字母之类的东西。她用这种浪漫的口吻谈论它，说这是最后的遗迹，最后的留存，是我们从那个家庭得到的最后的东西。无论事实是否如此，无论那个家庭是否幸存，我们都不一定知道。

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:03.510
<v Ira Glass>So for her, does Jewishness connote a simpler, more innocent time?
<v Ira Glass>所以对她来说，犹太属性暗示了一个更简单、更纯真的时代吗？

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:05.210
<v Erin Einhorn>I think probably, yes.
<v Erin Einhorn>我想很可能是的。

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:10.780
<v Ira Glass>And how strange that the time that it connotes is 1930s eastern Europe.
<v Ira Glass>多么奇怪啊，它所暗示的时代竟然是20世纪30年代的东欧。

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:27.370
<v Erin Einhorn>But you always have a romantic idea of what life was before you experienced it. And what was in Poland at that time, but Jews? It's something from the past. It's something magical and mystical.
<v Erin Einhorn>但在你真正体验之前，你总是会对生活有一种浪漫的想法。那个时期的波兰除了犹太人还能有什么？这是一种过去的东西。它是一种魔法般的、神秘的东西。

00:17:27.370 --> 00:17:32.170
<v Ira Glass>It's funny how being gone can make it magical and mystical.
<v Ira Glass>很有趣，一个人不在了，反而会让它变得充满魔法和神秘色彩。

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:58.110
<v Erin Einhorn>Yeah. And that's why these Jewish-themed cafes are all designed like 19th-century living rooms. They used old, white, lace tablecloths and antique furniture and antique frames on the portraits on the walls. It's got a poignant, melodramatic flair to it that the artistic types are drawn to.
<v Erin Einhorn>是的。这就是为什么这些犹太主题咖啡馆的设计都像是19世纪的客厅。他们使用了陈旧的白色蕾丝桌布、古董家具和墙上肖像画的古董框架。它带有一种艺术类型的人所向往的凄美、夸张的情调。

00:17:58.110 --> 00:18:08.480
<v Ira Glass>Was it just that the people who you were getting to know were fascinated by Jewish culture, or were they actually looking for their own Jewish roots? Was there a sense of, oh, maybe I'm part Jewish?
<v Ira Glass>仅仅是因为你结识的人对犹太文化着迷吗，还是说他们真的在寻找自己的犹太血统？有没有一种感觉，噢，也许我有一部分犹太血统？

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:44.410
<v Erin Einhorn>There is an absolute trend among certain young Poles to go try and find the Jewish ancestor in the closet. If you actually find a Jewish relative, it's something that distinguishes you from everybody else. Not only is the country largely homogeneous ethnically, but under communism, there were restricted colors of paint and restricted kinds of architecture. And everybody had the same sort of job and the same sort of house and the same sort of life. And it was all very same and really bland.
<v Erin Einhorn>在某些波兰年轻人中，绝对存在一种试图去寻找“壁橱里的犹太祖先”的趋势。如果你真的能找到一位犹太亲戚，那是一件让你与众不同的事情。不仅这个国家在种族上基本同质，而且在共产主义下，油漆颜色和建筑风格都是受限制的。每个人都有同样的工作、同样的房子、同样的生活。这一切都非常千篇一律，真的很平淡。

00:18:44.410 --> 00:19:05.430
<v Erin Einhorn>And this young culture wants to try and distinguish themselves from the previous generations, and try and show we're different. We're Western, we're capitalistic, I don't know, ethnically different. And so if you can actually prove that you are, in fact, ethnically different, well, that's taking it to the next level.
<v Erin Einhorn>而这种年轻文化想要努力与前几代人区分开来，想要展示我们是不一样的。我们是西方的，资本主义的，我不知道，种族上是不同的。所以如果你能证明你确实在种族上与众不同，好吧，那真是更上一层楼了。

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:12.330
<v Ira Glass>So as somebody who actually is Jewish, when you would explain to people that you're actually Jewish, was it suddenly like you were a celebrity?
<v Ira Glass>所以作为一名真正的犹太人，当你向人们解释你其实是犹太人时，感觉就像你是位名人吗？

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:36.790
<v Erin Einhorn>I don't think I was a celebrity, but it was like I just told them I came back from Mars. They want to hear everything about it. And actually, one of the things I think I said I heard all the time, people would hear I was a Jew. I'd be at a party. I would tell them what I was doing in Poland. And they'd say, "Oh my God. Wow. Really? You're a Jew? Wow, that's so exciting. My grandmother's best friend was a Jew." It's like every Polish grandmother had a Jew for a best friend.
<v Erin Einhorn>我不觉得我是名人，但这感觉就像我刚刚告诉他们我从火星回来一样。他们想听关于它的一切。事实上，我想我一直听到的事情之一是，人们听说我是犹太人。我会在派对上。我会告诉他们我在波兰做什么。他们会说：“噢，我的天。哇。真的吗？你是犹太人？哇，那太令人激动了。我祖母最好的朋友就是犹太人。”就像每位波兰祖母都有个犹太好朋友一样。

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:42.700
<v Ira Glass>That's such a weird variation on "some of my best friends are Jewish." "Some of my grandmother's best friends were Jewish."
<v Ira Glass>这真是一种对“我最好的朋友中有一些是犹太人”的奇怪变体。“我祖母最好的朋友中就有犹太人。”

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:58.870
<v Erin Einhorn>And it could be legitimate because their grandmothers were children just before the war. And especially if they were in a place like Krakow, which was urban and educated, there were lots and lots of Jews. The schools were integrated. Yeah, maybe. Maybe they aren't lying or-- and they tell their grandchildren about it.
<v Erin Einhorn>这也可能是真的，因为他们的祖母在战前还是孩子。特别是如果她们生活在克拉科夫这样的城市且受过教育的地方，那里有很多很多犹太人。学校是融合的。也许吧。也许他们没在撒谎——他们把这些告诉了孙辈。

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:18.650
<v Erin Einhorn>When you never meet a Jew, you never have a problem with a Jew. You never have a conflict. And all the things that may or may not have threatened their grandparents or their great-grandparents or the things that may have defined several hundred years of Polish-Jewish relations are moot. All they have is the romance, and the memory, and none of the things that create problems.
<v Erin Einhorn>当你从没见过犹太人，你就从没跟犹太人发生过矛盾。你从没发生过冲突。而那些可能威胁到他们祖父母或曾祖父母的事情，或者是那些可能定义了波兰与犹太人几百年关系的事情，现在都变得无关紧要了。他们所拥有的只有浪漫和记忆，而没有那些制造麻烦的东西。

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:27.850
<v Ira Glass>Do you think it's possible for Poles to honor Jews in a way that wouldn't feel a little bit creepy or disturbing to Jews?
<v Ira Glass>你认为波兰人有可能以一种不会让犹太人感到有点毛骨悚然或不安的方式来纪念犹太人吗？

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:56.050
<v Erin Einhorn>Yeah, I think I do have this suspicion, I guess, that I can't seem to get past. When people would come up to me and say, "Oh, yeah, my grandmother's best friend was a Jew," there's this part of me that would want to say, "Yeah? And what did Grandma do when they came to take away her friend?" Granted, Grandma was a nine-year-old girl, so she did nothing.
<v Erin Einhorn>是的，我想我确实有这种怀疑，我想我无法摆脱。当人们走过来对我说：“噢，是的，我祖母最好的朋友是犹太人”时，我内心有一部分会想说：“是吗？当他们要把她的朋友带走时，奶奶做了什么？”当然，奶奶当时是个九岁的小女孩，所以她什么也没做。

00:20:56.050 --> 00:21:08.300
<v Erin Einhorn>I wish I could view it not cynically. I wish that I were that open-minded and not so biased and prejudicial. But I'm a product of my own history.
<v Erin Einhorn>我希望我能不那么愤世嫉俗地看待它。我希望我能思想开放，不那么偏执和带有偏见。但我本身就是我自己历史的产物。

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:24.700
<v Erin Einhorn>And my mother had this crazy-- she had five mothers by the time she was nine because she was tossed around. And my grandmother was killed. And my grandfather was in Auschwitz. So you just can't set aside that context and that history, as much as you can try.
<v Erin Einhorn>我母亲有那种疯狂的经历——她在九岁之前就有五个母亲，因为她被到处转送。我祖母被杀了。我祖父在奥斯威辛。所以你无法抛开那个背景和那段历史，尽管你可以尝试。

00:21:24.700 --> 00:22:48.100
<v Ira Glass>Erin Einhorn is a reporter for the New York Daily News. She's finished her book about the family that hid her mother from the Holocaust, and she's looking for a publisher. The family, it turns out, did not just do it for the money. One of them burst into tears when Erin showed up at his door. Coming up, when your trivet does the talking, and you get ignored, and David Sedaris on love. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>艾琳·艾因霍恩是《纽约每日新闻》的记者。她写完了关于那个在二战中隐藏她母亲免遭大屠杀的家庭的书，正在寻找出版商。事实证明，这个家庭并不仅仅是为了钱。当艾琳出现在他的门口时，其中一个人热泪盈眶。接下来，当你的隔热垫在说话，而你被忽视时，以及大卫·塞达里斯谈论爱情。从芝加哥公共广播电台和公共广播国际台，我们节目继续。

00:22:48.100 --> 00:23:16.900
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week, of course, we choose some theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Fake ID, an idea so simple, a theme so simple that, for a change, I do not have to explain it. We have arrived at act two of our program, Act Two, This Blessed House. In this act, we bring you a piece of short fiction from Jhumpa Lahiri, a story whose similarities to Erin's story about Poland will become all too clear. Mira Nair reads this story for us.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，并为您带来各种不同类型的相关故事。今天的节目，“假身份”，一个如此简单的主题，简单到我甚至不需要解释它。我们进入节目的第二幕，“这个受祝福的家”。在这一幕中，我们为您带来Jhumpa Lahiri的一篇短篇小说，这个故事与艾琳关于波兰的故事之间的相似之处将变得非常清晰。米拉·奈尔为我们阅读了这个故事。

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:37.250
<v Mira Nair>They discovered the first one in a cupboard above the stove, beside an unopened bottle of malt vinegar. "Guess what I found?" Twinkle walked into the living room, lined from end to end with taped-up packing boxes, waving the vinegar in one hand and a white porcelain effigy of Christ, roughly the same size as the vinegar bottle, in the other.
<v Mira Nair>他们在炉子上方的一个橱柜里，在一瓶未开封的麦芽醋旁边发现了第一个。“猜猜我发现了什么？”特温克尔走进客厅，房间里排满了贴着胶带的包装箱，一只手挥舞着醋瓶，另一只手拿着一个白色的瓷制基督像，大小和醋瓶差不多。

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:51.330
<v Mira Nair>Sanjeev looked up. He was kneeling on the floor, marking, with ripped bits of a Post-it, patches on the baseboard that needed to be retouched with paint. "Throw it away." "Which?" she said. "Both."
<v Mira Nair>桑杰夫抬起头。他正跪在地上，用撕下的便利贴碎片标记着底板上需要重新补漆的地方。“把它扔掉。”“扔哪个？”她说。“两个都扔。”

00:23:51.330 --> 00:24:13.570
<v Mira Nair>"But I can cook something with the vinegar. It's brand new." "You've never cooked anything with vinegar." "I'll look something up in one of those books we got for our wedding." Sanjeev turned back to the baseboard to replace a Post-it strap that had fallen to the floor. "Check the expiration, and at the very least, get rid of that idiotic statue." "But it could be worth something, who knows?"
<v Mira Nair>“但我可以用这醋做饭啊。它是全新的。”“你从来没用过醋做饭。”“我会在我们结婚时收到的那些书里查查。”桑杰夫转过身继续去处理底板，更换掉落到地上的便利贴条。“检查一下保质期，至少把那个愚蠢的雕像扔掉。”“但它可能值点钱，谁知道呢？”

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:34.620
<v Mira Nair>She turned it upside down, then stroked with her index finger the minuscule, frozen folds of its robes. "It's pretty." "We're not Christian," Sanjeev said. Lately, he had begun noticing the need to state the obvious to Twinkle. The day before, he had to tell her that if she dragged her end of the bureau, instead of lifting it, the parquet floor would scratch.
<v Mira Nair>她把它倒过来，然后用食指轻轻抚摸着它长袍上微小的、凝固的褶皱。“它很漂亮。”“我们不是基督徒，”桑杰夫说。最近，他开始发现有必要对特温克尔说一些显而易见的话。前一天，他不得不告诉她，如果她拖着五斗橱的那一端走，而不是抬起来，拼花地板就会被刮伤。

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:56.910
<v Mira Nair>She shrugged. "No, we're not Christian. We're good little Hindus." She planted a kiss on top of Christ's head, then placed the statue on top of the fireplace mantle, which needed, Sanjeev observed, to be dusted.
<v Mira Nair>她耸了耸肩。“不，我们不是基督徒。我们是好的印度教徒。”她在基督头的顶部吻了一下，然后把雕像放在了壁炉台面上，桑杰夫注意到那上面需要掸掸灰尘了。

00:24:56.910 --> 00:25:36.530
<v Mira Nair>By the end of the week, the mantle had still not been dusted. It had, however, come to serve as the display shelf for a sizable collection of Christian paraphernalia. There was a 3-D postcard of Saint Francis done in four colors, which Twinkle had found taped to the back of the medicine cabinet, and a wooden cross keychain which Sanjeev had stepped on with bare feet as he was installing extra shelving in Twinkle's study. There was a framed paint-by-number of the three wise men against a black velvet background tucked in the linen closet. There was also a tile trivet depicting a blond, unbearded Jesus delivering a sermon on a mountaintop left in one of the drawers of the built-in china cabinet in the dining room.
<v Mira Nair>到周末结束时，台面还是没掸灰。然而，它却成了各种基督徒小物件的大型陈列架。有一个特温克尔在药柜后面发现的四色圣方济各3D明信片，还有一个木制十字架钥匙链，桑杰夫在特温克尔的书房安装额外的架子时，光着脚踩在了上面。在亚麻布柜里藏着一幅以黑色天鹅绒为背景的数字油画，画着三位智者。在餐厅内置中式橱柜的一个抽屉里，还留着一块瓷砖隔热垫，上面描绘着一个金发、没留胡子的耶稣在山顶上布道。

00:25:36.530 --> 00:26:24.820
<v Mira Nair>"Do you think the previous owners were born-agains?" asked Twinkle, making room the next day for a small, plastic snow-filled dome, containing a miniature nativity scene, found behind the pipes of the kitchen sink. Sanjeev was organizing his engineering texts from MIT in alphabetical order on a bookshelf, though it had been several years since he had needed to consult any of them. At 33, he had a secretary of his own and a dozen people working under his supervision who gladly supplied him with any information he needed. Still, the presence of his college books in the room reminded him of a time in his life he recalled with fondness, when he would walk each evening across the Mass Avenue bridge to order Mughlai chicken with spinach from his favorite Indian restaurant on the other side of the Charles and return to his dorm room to write out clean copies of his problem sets.
<v Mira Nair>“你觉得前房主是重生基督徒吗？”特温克尔第二天问道，她正在为一个小小的塑料圆顶挪地方，里面装着一个在厨房水槽水管后面发现的微型耶稣诞生场景。桑杰夫正在书架上按字母顺序整理他从麻省理工学院毕业时的工程教材，尽管他已经好几年不需要查阅其中的任何一本了。33岁时，他有了自己的秘书，手下有十几个人工作，他们乐意为他提供他需要的任何信息。不过，他在房间里看到的这些大学书籍提醒了他一段他怀念的时光，那时他每天晚上都要走过马萨诸塞大道桥，去查尔斯河对岸他最喜欢的印度餐馆点一份玛莎拉菠菜鸡，然后回到宿舍写下干净的习题作业副本。

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:38.880
<v Mira Nair>"Or perhaps it's an attempt to convert people," Twinkle mused. "Clearly, the scheme has succeeded in your case," Sanjeev said. She disregarded him, shaking the little plastic dome so that the snow swirled over the manger.
<v Mira Nair>“或者这可能是一种试图转化人们的尝试，”特温克尔若有所思地说。“显然，这个计划在你身上成功了，”桑杰夫说。她没理会他，摇晃着那个小塑料圆顶，让雪花在马槽上方飞舞。

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:58.570
<v Mira Nair>Sanjeev studied the items on the mantle. It puzzled him that each was, in its own way, so silly. Clearly, they lacked a sense of sacredness. He was further puzzled that Twinkle, who normally displayed good taste, was so charmed. These objects meant something to Twinkle, but they meant nothing to him. They irritated him.
<v Mira Nair>桑杰夫研究着台面上的物品。令他困惑的是，每一个都显得那么愚蠢。显然，它们缺乏一种神圣感。更让他困惑的是，一向品味不错的特温克尔竟然会被这些东西迷住。这些物件对特温克尔有意义，对他来说却毫无意义。它们让他恼火。

00:26:58.570 --> 00:27:15.370
<v Mira Nair>"We should call the realtor, tell them there's all this nonsense left behind. Tell him to take it away." "Oh, Sanj," Twinkle groaned. "Please, I would feel terrible throwing them away. Obviously, they were important to the people who used to live here. It would feel, I don't know, I mean, sacrilegious or something?"
<v Mira Nair>“我们应该给房地产中介打电话，告诉他们还有这些乱七八糟的东西留下来。告诉他把它带走。”“噢，桑杰，”特温克尔呻吟道。“拜托，扔掉它们我会觉得很难过。显然，它们对以前住在这里的人很重要。这样做会显得，我不知道，我的意思是，亵渎神明之类的？”

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:37.240
<v Mira Nair>"If they're so precious, then why were they hidden all over the house? Why didn't they take them with them?" "There must be others," Twinkle said. Her eyes roamed the bare, off-white walls of the room, as if there were other things concealed behind the plaster. "What else do you think we'll find?"
<v Mira Nair>“如果它们那么珍贵，为什么它们被藏在房子的各个角落？为什么他们不带走？”“肯定还有别的，”特温克尔说。她的目光扫视着房间里光秃秃的灰白色墙壁，仿佛石膏后面还藏着别的东西。“你觉得我们还会发现什么？”

00:27:37.240 --> 00:28:10.540
<v Mira Nair>Nearly a week had passed before they discovered, one Saturday afternoon, a larger than life-sized watercolor poster of Christ, weeping translucent tears the size of peanut shells and sporting a crown of thorns, rolled up behind a radiator in the guest bedroom. Sanjeev had mistaken it for a window shade. "Oh, we must, we simply must put it up. It's too spectacular." Twinkle lit a cigarette and began to smoke it with relish, waving it around Sanjeev's head as if it were a conductor's baton, as Mahler's Fifth Symphony roared from the stereo downstairs.
<v Mira Nair>几乎过了一个星期，他们在一个周六下午发现了一张比真人还大的基督水彩海报，基督流着花生壳大小的透明泪水，带着荆棘冠，卷在客房的散热器后面。桑杰夫把它误认为是窗帘。“噢，我们必须，我们一定要把它挂起来。它太壮观了。”特温克尔点了一支烟，津津有味地抽着，像指挥棒一样在桑杰夫头顶挥舞，楼下的音响里咆哮着马勒第五交响曲。

00:28:10.540 --> 00:28:43.440
<v Mira Nair>"Now look, I will tolerate for now your little Biblical menagerie in the living room. But I refuse to have this," he said, flicking at one of the painted peanut tears, "displayed in our home." Twinkle stared at him, the smoke emerging in two thin, blue streams from her nostrils. She rolled up the poster slowly, securing it with one of the elastic bands she always wore around her wrist for tying back her thick, unruly hair, streaked here and there with henna. "I'm going to put it in my study," she informed him. "That way, you don't have to look at it."
<v Mira Nair>“现在听着，我暂时可以容忍你在客厅里的那些小圣经珍品。但我拒绝让这个，”他说着，弹了一下其中一颗彩绘的花生泪水，“出现在我们的家里。”特温克尔盯着他，烟从她的鼻孔中冒出两缕细细的蓝色烟流。她慢慢卷起海报，用她总是戴在手腕上用来系她浓密、凌乱头发的一根橡皮筋固定住，头发上还杂乱地染着指甲花。她通知他：“我要把它放在我的书房里。那样你就不用看了。”

