00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:41.300
<v Mira Nair>The strident voices had subsided to an even murmur. It occurred to Sanjeev that he had the house all to himself. The music had ended, and he could hear, if he concentrated, the hum of the refrigerator and the rustle of the last leaves on the trees outside. With one flick of his hand, he could snap the ladder back on its spring into the ceiling, and they would have no way of getting down, unless he were to pull the chain and let them.
<v Mira Nair>刺耳的声音已经平息为均匀的低语。桑杰夫想到他现在整个房子里只有他一个人。音乐结束了，他如果集中注意力，能听到冰箱的嗡嗡声和外面树上最后几片落叶的沙沙声。只要手一弹，他就能把梯子弹回天花板里，除非他拉动链子让他们下来，否则他们就没法下来。

00:46:41.300 --> 00:47:09.840
<v Mira Nair>He thought of all the things he could do, undisturbed. He could sweep Twinkle's menagerie into a garbage bag and get in the car and drive it all to the dump, and tear down the poster of weeping Jesus, and take a hammer to the Virgin Mary while he was at it. Then he would return to the empty house. He could easily clear up the cups and plates in an hour's time, and pour himself a gin and tonic, and eat a plate of warmed rice, and listen to his new Bach CD, while reading the liner notes so as to understand it properly.
<v Mira Nair>他想到了所有他可以做的、不受干扰的事情。他可以把特温克尔的“珍品”扫进垃圾袋，开车去垃圾场，撕掉哭泣耶稣的海报，顺便用锤子砸烂圣母玛利亚像。然后他回到空荡荡的房子。他可以轻松地在一小时内清理掉杯子和盘子，给自己倒一杯金汤力，吃一盘加热过的米饭，听他的新巴赫CD，同时阅读封套说明以便正确理解。

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:23.010
<v Mira Nair>He nudged the ladder slightly, but it was sturdily planted against the door. Budging it would require some effort. "My God, I need a cigarette," Twinkle exclaimed from above.
<v Mira Nair>他轻轻推了推梯子，但它稳稳地顶在门上。挪动它需要一点力气。“天哪，我需要抽根烟，”特温克尔在上面惊呼。

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:34.960
<v Mira Nair>Sanjeev felt knots forming at the back of his neck. He felt dizzy. He needed to lie down. He walked towards the bedroom, but stopped short when he saw Twinkle's shoes facing him in the doorway.
<v Mira Nair>桑杰夫感觉到脖子后面形成了结。他感到头晕。他需要躺下。他走向卧室，但在看到特温克尔的鞋子对着他摆在门口时停住了。

00:47:34.960 --> 00:48:03.470
<v Mira Nair>He thought of her slipping them on her feet. But instead of feeling irritated, as he had ever since they'd moved into the house together, he felt a pang of anticipation at the thought of her rushing unsteadily down the winding staircase in them, scratching the floor a bit in her path. The pang intensified as he thought of her rushing to the bathroom to brighten her lipstick, and eventually rushing to get people their coats, and finally rushing to the cherrywood table when the last guest had left to begin opening their housewarming presents.
<v Mira Nair>他想到她穿上它们的样子。但与其感到恼火，像他们搬进这房子以来那样，他感到一种期待的刺痛，一想到她穿着它们摇摇晃晃地沿着回旋楼梯冲下来，在路上把地板刮出一点痕迹，那刺痛就加剧了。当他想到她冲进洗手间补口红，最终冲去帮人们拿外套，并最终在最后一位客人离开时冲到樱桃木桌子旁开始打开暖房礼物时，那种刺痛更加强烈了。

00:48:03.470 --> 00:48:31.420
<v Mira Nair>It was the same pang that he used to feel before they were married, when he would hang up the phone after one of their conversations, or when he would drive back from the airport, wondering which ascending plane in the sky was hers. "Sanj, you won't believe this." She emerged with her back to him, her hands over her head, the tops of her bare shoulder blades perspiring, supporting something still hidden from view. "You got it, Twinkle?" someone asked. "Yes, you can let go."
<v Mira Nair>那种刺痛和他婚前感到的一样，当时他在结束通话后挂断电话，或者当他从机场开车回来，猜测天空中哪架飞机是她的时候。“桑杰，你绝对不会相信这个。”她背对他冒出来，双手放在头顶，赤裸的肩胛骨顶部渗出汗水，支撑着某种依然看不见的东西。“你拿到了，特温克尔？”有人问。“是的，你们可以放手了。”

00:48:31.420 --> 00:49:03.370
<v Mira Nair>Now he saw that her hands were wrapped around it, a solid silver bust of Christ, the head easily three times the size of his own. It had a patrician bump on its nose, magnificent, curly hair that rested atop a pronounced collarbone, and a broad forehead that reflected in miniature the walls and doors and lampshades around them. Its expression was confident, as if assured of its devotees, the unyielding lips sensuous and full. It was also sporting Nora's feather hat.
<v Mira Nair>现在他看到她的手环绕着它，一个实心的银色基督半身像，头颅的大小轻易就是他自己的三倍。它有一个贵族式的鼻梁，浓密的卷发停在一个凸出的锁骨上，宽阔的前额微缩地映射出周围的墙壁、门和灯罩。它的表情充满自信，仿佛确信自己的虔诚者，不屈的嘴唇丰满而感性。它还戴着诺拉的羽毛帽。

00:49:03.370 --> 00:49:21.760
<v Mira Nair>As Twinkle descended, Sanjeev put his hands around her waist to balance her. And he relieved her of the bust when she had reached the ground. It weighed a good 30 pounds. The others began lowering themselves slowly, exhausted from the hunt. Some trickled downstairs in search of a fresh drink.
<v Mira Nair>当特温克尔下来时，桑杰夫双手搂住她的腰帮她平衡。当她落地时，他帮她接过了半身像。它足有30磅重。其他人开始慢慢降下来，狩猎让他们精疲力竭。有些人流向楼下寻找新鲜饮料。

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:41.660
<v Mira Nair>She took a breath, raised her eyebrows, crossed her fingers. "Would you mind terribly if we displayed it on the mantle, just for tonight? I know you hate it." He did hate it. He hated its immensity, and its flawless, polished surface, and its undeniable value. He hated that it was in his house and that he owned it.
<v Mira Nair>她深吸一口气，扬起眉毛，交叉着手指。“如果你不介意的话，我们把它放在壁炉台面上展示，只展示今晚，好吗？我知道你讨厌它。”他确实讨厌它。他讨厌它的巨大，讨厌它毫无瑕疵、闪闪发光的表面，讨厌它不可否认的价值。他讨厌它在他的房子里，讨厌他拥有它。

00:49:41.660 --> 00:49:55.350
<v Mira Nair>Unlike the other things they'd found, this contained dignity, solemnity, beauty even. But to his surprise, these qualities made him hate it all the more. Most of all, he hated it because he knew that Twinkle loved it.
<v Mira Nair>与他们发现的其他东西不同，这个东西包含着尊严、庄重，甚至美丽。但令他惊讶的是，这些品质让他更加讨厌它。最重要的是，他讨厌它，因为他知道特温克尔爱它。

00:49:55.350 --> 00:50:30.240
<v Mira Nair>"I'll keep it in my study from tomorrow," Twinkle added. "I promise." She would never put it in her study, he knew. For the rest of their days together, she would keep it on the center of the mantle, flanked on either side by the rest of the menagerie. Each time they had guests, Twinkle would explain how she had found it, and they would admire her as they listened. He gazed at the crushed rose petals in her hair, at the pearl and sapphire choker at her throat, at the sparkly crimson polish on her toes. He decided that these were among the things that made Prabal think she was wow.
<v Mira Nair>“从明天开始，我就把它放在我的书房里，”特温克尔补充道。“我保证。”他知道她永远不会把它放进书房。在他们共度的余生里，她会把它放在壁炉台的中心，两边陈列着其余的那些珍品。每次有客人来，特温克尔都会解释她是怎样发现它的，而他们会在聆听时赞美她。他凝视着她发丝里压碎的玫瑰花瓣，她喉咙上的珍珠蓝宝石颈圈，她脚趾上闪闪发光的深红色指甲油。他认定这些就是让普拉巴尔认为她太棒了的原因。

00:50:30.240 --> 00:50:57.260
<v Mira Nair>His head ached from gin, and his arms ached from the weight of the statue. He said, "I put your shoes in the bedroom." Twinkle gave his elbow a little squeeze and headed for the living room. "Thanks, but my feet are killing me." Sanjeev pressed the massive silver face to his ribs, careful not to let the feather hat slip, and followed her.
<v Mira Nair>他的头因为金酒而作痛，胳膊因为雕像的重量而酸痛。他说：“我把你的鞋放卧室了。”特温克尔捏了捏他的手肘，向客厅走去。“谢谢，但我的脚疼得要命。”桑杰夫把厚重的银色脸庞紧贴在自己的肋骨上，小心不让羽毛帽滑落，跟着她走了过去。

00:50:57.260 --> 00:51:13.760
<v Ira Glass>Mira Nair, reading a slightly abridged version of Jhumpa Lahiri's story, "This Blessed House," from the book The Interpreter of Maladies, which is such a great book, also winner of the Pulitzer Prize. Mira Nair, who read, is the director of Monsoon Wedding, Mississippi Masala, Vanity Fair, and other films.
<v Ira Glass>米拉·奈尔，阅读了Jhumpa Lahiri的《这个受祝福的家》的一个略微删减的版本，选自那本伟大的书《疾病解说者》，也是普利策奖得主。阅读者米拉·奈尔是《季风婚宴》、《密西西比马萨拉》、《名利场》和其他电影的导演。

00:51:13.760 --> 00:52:01.970
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "HALLELUJAH" BY JOHN CALE]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 约翰·凯尔的“哈利路亚”]

00:52:01.970 --> 00:52:24.420
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, The Lie That Binds. You could argue that it is impossible to stay in any relationship for very long without sometimes traveling under fake papers, without sometimes concealing what you think. I say this, and I don't mean it cynically. I mean being in love sometimes means not saying what is going through your head. David Sedaris lives in Paris with his boyfriend, Hugh, and he provides this case example.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“束缚的谎言”。你可以争论说，如果不偶尔使用假证件，如果不偶尔隐藏你的真实想法，想要在任何关系中长时间维持下去是不可能的。我这样说，并不是愤世嫉俗。我的意思是，身处爱中，有时意味着不说出你脑子里真正想的东西。大卫·塞达里斯和他的男友休住在巴黎，他提供了这个案例。

00:52:24.420 --> 00:52:39.830
<v David Sedaris>Last night, Hugh and I went around the corner to see The End of the Affair, a Neil Jordan adaptation of the Graham Greene novel. He sobbed from beginning to end. And by the time we left the theater, the poor thing was completely dehydrated.
<v David Sedaris>昨晚，休和我去了街角看《爱到尽头》，尼尔·乔丹改编的格雷厄姆·格林的同名小说。他从头哭到尾。当我们离开电影院时，那个可怜的东西完全脱水了。

00:52:39.830 --> 00:53:08.920
<v David Sedaris>I asked if he always cried during comedies, and he accused me of being grossly insensitive, a charge I'm trying to plea bargain down to a simple obnoxious. Looking back, I should've known better than to accompany Hugh to a love story. Such movies are always a danger as, unlike battling aliens or going undercover to track a serial killer, falling in love is something most adults have actually experienced at some point in their lives.
<v David Sedaris>我问他是不是看喜剧时也总是哭，他指责我极其麻木不仁，这个指控我正试图辩解为仅仅是讨厌。回想起来，我早该知道不该陪休去看爱情片。这类电影总是很危险，因为与对抗外星人或卧底追踪连环杀手不同，坠入爱河是大多数成年人实际上在生命中的某个阶段都经历过的事情。

00:53:08.920 --> 00:53:31.970
<v David Sedaris>The theme is universal and encourages the viewer to make a number of unhealthy comparisons, leading to the question, "Why can't our lives be like that?" It's a box best left unopened. And its avoidance explains the continued popularity of vampire epics and martial arts extravaganzas.
<v David Sedaris>这个主题是普遍的，鼓励观众进行许多不健康的比较，从而引发问题：“为什么我们的生活不能像那样？”这是一个最好不要打开的盒子。而避免触碰它，解释了吸血鬼史诗和武术奇观依然流行的原因。

00:53:31.970 --> 00:53:55.920
<v David Sedaris>The End of the Affair made me look like an absolute toad. The movie's voracious couple was played by Ralph Fiennes and Julianne Moore, who did everything but eat one another. Their love was doomed and clandestine, and even when the bombs were falling, they looked radiant. Something about Julianne Moore and Ralph Fiennes put me on the defensive.
<v David Sedaris>《爱到尽头》让我看起来像个十足的癞蛤蟆。电影中那对贪婪的情侣由拉尔夫·费因斯和朱丽安·摩尔扮演，他们几乎无所不为，甚至要吃掉对方。他们的爱注定失败且秘而不宣，即使炸弹落下，他们看起来依然光彩照人。朱丽安·摩尔和拉尔夫·费因斯之间某种东西让我陷入了防御状态。

00:53:55.920 --> 00:54:10.980
<v David Sedaris>I'm not as unfeeling as Hugh accuses me of being, but things change once you've been together for 10 years. They rarely make movies about long-term couples and for good reason. Our lives are boring.
<v David Sedaris>我不像休指责的那样麻木，但一旦你们在一起十年，事情就会改变。他们很少拍摄关于长期伴侣的电影，这有很好的理由。我们的生活很无聊。

00:54:10.980 --> 00:54:48.460
<v David Sedaris>The courtship had its moments, but now we've become the predictable part two no one in his right mind would ever pay to see. Hugh and I have been together for so long that in order to arouse extraordinary passion, we need to engage in actual physical combat. Once, he hit me on the back of the head with a broken wine glass, and I fell to the floor, pretending to be unconscious. That was romantic, or would have been had he rushed to my side, rather than stepping over my body to fetch the dustpan.
<v David Sedaris>求爱期或许有一些高光时刻，但现在我们已经成了没人会想花钱去看的、可预测的续集。休和我在一起太久了，以至于为了激发出非凡的激情，我们需要进行实际的肢体战斗。有一次，他用破酒杯砸我的后脑勺，我倒在地上，假装昏迷。那是浪漫的，或者说，如果他当时赶过来照顾我，而不是跨过我的身体去拿簸箕的话，那本来会是浪漫的。

00:54:48.460 --> 00:55:01.410
<v David Sedaris>Call me unimaginative, but I still can't think of anyone else I'd rather be with. On our worst days, I figure things will work themselves out. Otherwise, I really don't give it much thought.
<v David Sedaris>你可以说我缺乏想象力，但我依然想不出还有谁比他更适合我。在最糟糕的日子里，我心想事情总会解决的。否则，我真的不会对此多加思考。

00:55:01.410 --> 00:55:31.570
<v David Sedaris>Neither of us would ever publicly display affection. We're just not that type. We can't profess love without talking through hand puppets, and we'd never consciously sit down to discuss our relationship. These, to me, are good things. They were fine with Hugh as well until he saw that damn movie and was reminded that he has other options.
<v David Sedaris>我们两人都不会在公开场合展示亲密。我们不是那种类型。我们无法在不使用手偶的情况下表达爱意，我们也永远不会坐下来有意识地讨论我们的关系。对我来说，这些是好事。这对休来说也很好，直到他看了那部该死的电影，并被提醒他还有其他选择。

00:55:31.570 --> 00:55:56.100
<v David Sedaris>The picture ended at around 10:00. And afterwards, we went for coffees at a little place across the street from the Luxembourg Gardens. I was ready to wipe the movie out of my mind, but Hugh was still under its spell. He looked as though his life had not only passed him by, but paused along the way to spit in his face.
<v David Sedaris>电影在10点左右结束。之后，我们去卢森堡公园对面的一家小店喝咖啡。我准备把这部电影从脑子里擦掉，但休依然深受其影响。他看起来就好像他的人生不仅从他身边溜走，还在路上停下来吐在他脸上。

00:55:56.100 --> 00:56:20.360
<v David Sedaris>Our coffees arrived, and as he blew his nose into a napkin, I encouraged him to look on the bright side. "Listen," I said, "We maybe don't live in wartime London, but in terms of the occasional bomb scare, Paris is a pretty close second. We both love taxidermy. We take vacations to fun places like Croatia. What more could you possibly want?"
<v David Sedaris>我们的咖啡到了，当他对着餐巾纸擤鼻涕时，我鼓励他看光明的一面。“听着，”我说，“我们或许不住在战时的伦敦，但就偶尔的炸弹恐慌而言，巴黎是相当接近的第二名。我们都喜欢标本制作。我们去克罗地亚这样有趣的地方度假。你还想怎么样？”

00:56:20.360 --> 00:56:30.790
<v David Sedaris>What more could he want? It was an incredibly stupid question. And when he failed to answer, I was reminded of just how lucky I truly am.
<v David Sedaris>他还能想要什么？这是一个极其愚蠢的问题。当他没能回答时，我意识到自己是多么幸运。

00:56:30.790 --> 00:57:25.340
<v David Sedaris>Movie characters might chase one another through the fog, or race down the stairs of burning buildings, but that's just for beginners. Real love amounts to withholding the truth, even when you're offered the perfect opportunity to hurt somebody's feelings. I wanted to say something to this effect, but my hand puppets were back home in their drawer. Instead, I pulled my chair a few inches closer, and we sat silently at our little table on the square, looking for all the world like two people in love.
<v David Sedaris>电影角色可能会在雾中追逐对方，或从着火建筑的楼梯上赛跑，但这只是入门。真实的爱情等同于隐瞒真相，即使是在你可以完美伤到某人情感的时候。我本想说些类似的话，但我的手偶还在家里的抽屉里。相反，我把椅子拉近了几英寸，我们默默地坐在广场上的小桌前，看起来就像世界上两个相爱的人。

00:57:25.340 --> 00:57:35.830
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris is the author most recently of Dress Your Family in Corduroy and Denim . He also edited the collection Children Playing Before a Statue of Hercules , an anthology of outstanding stories.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯是《给你的家人穿上灯芯绒和丹宁》的作者。他还编辑了文集《在赫拉克勒斯雕像前玩耍的孩子》，这是一部优秀故事集。

00:57:35.830 --> 00:57:46.400
